 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np10-01",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "at non infelix animi Phoenissa",
   "trans": "but not the unhappy Phoenician queen",
   "line": "529",
   "body": "The opening at non sets Dido apart from the sleeping world described just before: everyone else rests, but she does not.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Epithet",
   "quote": "infelix",
   "trans": "unhappy",
   "line": "529",
   "body": "infelix returns from line 68: the doomed queen of the first half of the book is now near her end.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-03",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "solvitur in somnos oculisve aut pectore noctem accipit",
   "trans": "is released into sleep, or takes the night into her eyes or heart",
   "line": "530–531",
   "body": "Sleep is denied three ways, in her body, her eyes and her heart, so the sleeplessness seems total.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "ingeminant curae",
   "trans": "her cares redouble",
   "line": "531",
   "body": "ingeminant, \"they double\", shows her anxieties multiplying rather than calming in the night.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Sea imagery",
   "quote": "magnoque irarum fluctuat aestu",
   "trans": "she surges on a great tide of anger",
   "line": "532",
   "body": "Dido's emotions are a stormy sea, an image that suits a story of ships and sailing. Her love has turned into rage.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-06",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "resurgens saevit amor",
   "trans": "love rises again and rages",
   "line": "531–532",
   "body": "Love itself \"rages\" (saevit), a verb used of wild animals and storms, so love and anger have become one.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Deliberative question",
   "quote": "en, quid ago?",
   "trans": "well, what am I to do?",
   "line": "534",
   "body": "The present tense ago, where a subjunctive might be expected, makes the question sudden and urgent, as if she is speaking her thoughts aloud.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "rursusne procos inrisa priores experiar",
   "trans": "am I to try my former suitors again, a laughing stock?",
   "line": "534–535",
   "body": "inrisa, \"laughed at\", shows how much Dido fears humiliation. She once rejected these men, and now pictures them mocking her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "supplex",
   "trans": "as a suppliant",
   "line": "535",
   "body": "The proud queen imagines herself begging: supplex shows how far she has fallen.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-10",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Sarcasm",
   "quote": "quiane auxilio iuvat ante levatos",
   "trans": "because, of course, they are glad to have been helped before?",
   "line": "538",
   "body": "Dido's bitter irony shows her sense of betrayal: the Trojans she rescued are repaying her with desertion.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "bene apud memores veteris stat gratia facti",
   "trans": "gratitude for an old kindness stands firm among those who remember it",
   "line": "539",
   "body": "The sarcasm continues: Dido means the opposite, that the Trojans have no gratitude at all.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-12",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "quis me autem, fac velle, sinet",
   "trans": "but who, suppose I wanted to, would allow me?",
   "line": "540",
   "body": "Even the option of following Aeneas is closed off, as Dido answers her own question.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Self-address",
   "quote": "nescis heu, perdita",
   "trans": "don't you know, alas, ruined woman?",
   "line": "541",
   "body": "Dido turns on herself as perdita, \"ruined\" or \"lost\". The self-address shows a mind divided against itself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Allusion",
   "quote": "Laomedonteae sentis periuria gentis",
   "trans": "the perjuries of Laomedon's race",
   "line": "542",
   "body": "Dido brands all Trojans as liars, recalling the king of Troy who cheated the gods. The heavy, five-syllable adjective gives the insult weight.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-15",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "sola fuga nautas comitabor ovantes",
   "trans": "shall I go alone in flight with the rejoicing sailors?",
   "line": "543",
   "body": "sola is set against the crowd of cheering sailors, so the picture of Dido alone among the joyful Trojans is especially painful.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-16",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "quos Sidonia vix urbe revelli",
   "trans": "whom I barely tore away from the city of Sidon",
   "line": "545",
   "body": "revelli, \"tore away\", recalls Dido's flight from Tyre after her brother killed her husband: she cannot ask her people to uproot themselves again.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-17",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "pelago et ventis",
   "trans": "to the sea and the winds",
   "line": "546",
   "body": "Sea and wind recall the dangers the Tyrians already faced. The pairing makes the idea of another voyage feel exhausting and impossible.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Phoenissa",
   "quote": "Phoenissa",
   "trans": "the Phoenician woman",
   "line": "529",
   "body": "Dido came from Tyre in Phoenicia (modern Lebanon); Carthage was a Phoenician colony.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-19",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "The Nomads",
   "quote": "Nomadumque",
   "trans": "of the Nomads",
   "line": "535",
   "body": "The Numidians, the native peoples of north Africa around Carthage, among them Iarbas, whose offers of marriage Dido had refused.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np10-20",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Laomedon",
   "quote": "Laomedonteae",
   "trans": "of Laomedon",
   "line": "542",
   "body": "An early king of Troy who refused to pay Apollo and Neptune for building the city's walls, and later cheated Hercules. His broken promises became a byword for Trojan treachery.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 10,
 "questions": [
  "Dido's speech is a series of questions (534–546). What options does she consider, and why does she reject each one?",
  "How does Virgil contrast the peace of the night (the lines just before this section) with Dido's state of mind?",
  "Dido calls herself \"perdita\" (541). How far has she come from the queen we saw building Carthage in Book 1?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Between line 128 and line 529",
   "body": "<p>The set text jumps from the goddesses' plan to the end of Dido's story. In between, Dido and Aeneas meet in the cave and live openly as a couple, and Rumour spreads the news. The African king Iarbas, a rejected suitor, complains to Jupiter, who sends Mercury to remind Aeneas of his destiny in Italy. Aeneas prepares to leave secretly; Dido finds out and confronts him, and the two speeches that follow are among the most famous in Latin literature. He insists that he goes to Italy not of his own will. Dido, in despair, builds a pyre, pretending that she means to burn Aeneas' belongings in a magic rite.</p><p>Section 10 opens on the last night before the Trojans sail. Everything else in the world sleeps, but Dido cannot.</p>"
  }
 ]
}